# SteamHangar deployment configuration.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#   # edit .env -- at minimum set VAULT_API_KEY
#   docker compose up -d
#
# Compose reads .env from this directory automatically. NEVER COMMIT .env
# (the repo's .gitignore already excludes it) -- this .example file is the
# committed template and must never contain a real key.
#
# Every value below is optional EXCEPT VAULT_API_KEY. The defaults shown in
# comments are what deploy/compose.yaml falls back to if a line is missing.
#
# UPGRADE NOTE (2026-08-17 packaging work package): a dozen keys that used to
# be silently ignored if you set them here (Compose never forwarded them,
# `deploy/compose.yaml`'s own comments now say which ones and why) are
# forwarded and validated as of this update. If your existing `.env` already
# has a line for one of them with a typo, or a value `vault_api/config.py`'s
# strict grammar rejects (e.g. a `VAULT_MANIFEST_ORACLE_URL` with no scheme),
# it was harmless before and becomes vault-api refusing to start, with an
# explicit error message naming the bad key, after this update. That is the
# intended trade-off (a loud failure at boot beats a silent no-op forever),
# but it is a real behavior change on upgrade, not just a new feature.
#
# UPGRADE NOTE (2026-08-22, WP SWEEP-1 / docs/adr/0014-sweep-cached-and-auto-
# gc-default-on.md): VAULT_SWEEP_INCLUDE_CACHED and VAULT_AUTO_GC's own
# built-in defaults FLIPPED, together, on an explicit operator decision --
# `false`/`off` to `true`/`execute`. A same-day follow-up round (review round
# 2 of this same package) ALSO forwards VAULT_SCHEDULE_WINDOW (default
# `03:00-07:00`) and TZ (default `UTC`) for the first time -- without a
# window, the scheduler thread this pairing depends on never actually sweeps,
# so this line is not a separate, later change, it is the thing that makes
# the sweep half of the pairing above real rather than inert. If your
# existing `.env` never set any of these three, this update changes what
# your vault actually DOES the next time you recreate its containers: it
# starts sweeping nightly from 03:00-07:00 (UTC unless you also set TZ),
# widening that sweep to every game already sitting in the cache (installed
# anywhere or not), and it starts actually deleting the orphaned chunks that
# widening leaves behind -- where before this update nothing swept, widened,
# or deleted anything automatically. If your `.env` already sets any of the
# three explicitly, or you manage any of them via `PATCH /v1/settings`,
# nothing changes for that one -- an explicit value always wins over a
# changed default.
#
# To keep the exact pre-2026-08-22 behavior on a fresh `.env`, add ALL THREE
# lines below uncommented -- two is not enough: leaving VAULT_SCHEDULE_WINDOW
# at its new default still starts a nightly scheduler (an installed-only
# sweep, without the cached-apps widening or the auto-GC, but a real change
# from "no scheduler at all" through WP SWEEP-1's own first draft):
#   VAULT_SWEEP_INCLUDE_CACHED=false
#   VAULT_AUTO_GC=off
#   VAULT_SCHEDULE_WINDOW=
#
# That third line is BLANK, not omitted, and the blank must actually reach
# vault-api: review round 2 (blocker R2-B1) measured that the Compose
# forwarding line for VAULT_SCHEDULE_WINDOW originally used a substitution
# form that silently ignored an explicit blank value and rendered the
# 03:00-07:00 default anyway -- fixed in deploy/compose.yaml (the no-colon
# substitution form), and this recipe now actually works because of that
# fix, not merely because it looks right on paper.


# =============================================================================
# REQUIRED
# =============================================================================

# Shared secret every vault-api client (Android app, vault-agent) sends in the
# X-Api-Key header. There is no default: Compose refuses to start without it,
# and vault-api refuses to boot without it. Generate a long random value, e.g.
#     openssl rand -base64 36
#     python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(36))"
# and paste it after the `=` below. Treat it like a password; changing it
# means updating every client.
#
# Deliberately BLANK here, not a placeholder string (pre-freeze project
# review, finding B3): compose.yaml's `${VAULT_API_KEY:?...}` guard refuses
# an unset or EMPTY value, so a copied-but-unedited .env fails loudly at
# `docker compose up` -- whereas a placeholder like "changeme-..." would have
# passed that guard and booted a vault whose API key every reader of this
# public file already knows. deploy/README.md's "there is no default API key
# anywhere in this project" is only true while this line stays blank.
VAULT_API_KEY=


# =============================================================================
# vault-core (the cache)
# =============================================================================

# Which host address vault-core's port 80 is published on. Default: 0.0.0.0
# (all interfaces) -- correct for a LAN cache, because every Steam client has to
# reach it and the Steam client cannot authenticate.
#
# Set this to a DEDICATED IP if something else on the host already uses port 80
# (docs/PROJECT_PLAN.md §10). Do NOT solve a port clash by changing
# VAULT_CORE_PORT: the Steam client only ever asks for port 80, so a cache on
# 8080 is a cache nothing uses. Add an IP alias instead:
#     ip addr add 192.168.1.50/24 dev eth0     # (persist it via your distro)
#     VAULT_CORE_BIND=192.168.1.50
# and point your DNS rewrite / CACHE_IP at that address.
#VAULT_CORE_BIND=0.0.0.0

# Host port for vault-core. Only ever change this for testing -- see above.
#VAULT_CORE_PORT=80

# Upstream DNS resolver nginx uses to resolve Steam CDN hostnames at request
# time (ADR-0001 req 4). Default: 1.1.1.1.
# Change it if you must not send DNS to Cloudflare, or if your ISP/router
# resolver is meaningfully closer. Must be a plain IP address (validated at
# container start).
#
# WARNING, and read this even if you skipped everything else above: never
# point this at ANY resolver that rewrites *.steamcontent.com to this cache.
# vault-dns is the obvious case (it answers *.steamcontent.com with
# vault-core's own address by design), but the EXACT SAME failure hits an
# AdGuard Home or Pi-hole instance running on this same host, if you followed
# `deploy/README.md` "DNS: pick one of three modes" mode 1 and added the
# *.steamcontent.com rewrite there -- a very common homelab layout (AdGuard
# Home/Pi-hole as a SCALE/Unraid app right next to this stack). Point
# VAULT_RESOLVER at THAT resolver and vault-core asks it "where is
# steamcontent.com?", gets back vault-core's own address, and would proxy
# every cache miss into itself. vault-core catches this: at boot
# 40-vault-preflight.sh probes this resolver and refuses to start on a
# private answer; if the rewrite only appears later, every miss is answered
# 508 Loop Detected after one hop and nothing is cached -- the cache simply
# stops filling. Point this at a resolver that does NOT rewrite the zone:
# a public resolver (the default below), or your router/ISP resolver.
# The HTTPS passthrough below resolves through this same resolver.
#VAULT_RESOLVER=1.1.1.1


# HTTPS passthrough on port 443 (WP CORE-FIX-3, ADR-0020; deploy/README.md
# "Port 443: the HTTPS passthrough"). When your DNS rewrites
# *.steamcontent.com to this cache, EVERY connection to those names lands
# here -- including HTTPS ones. SteamPrefill fetches depot manifests over
# HTTPS from those names (and Steam clients may too); with nothing on 443
# they fail ("HttpRequestException ... while downloading manifests").
# vault-core answers 443 by reading only the TLS server name (SNI) and, for
# a *.steamcontent.com name, copying the encrypted bytes to Valve's real
# server unchanged: no certificate here, nothing decrypted, nothing cached,
# not counted against VAULT_UPSTREAM_RATE. Any other name, or no name, is
# closed at once -- it is not a general-purpose relay.
#
# VAULT_TLS_PASSTHROUGH: 1 (default) = listener on, 0 = off. Accepted:
# 1/true/on/yes and 0/false/off/no; anything else stops vault-core's boot.
# A blank value means the default (on) -- write 0 to switch it off.
#VAULT_TLS_PASSTHROUGH=1
#
# VAULT_TLS_BIND: the host address port 443 is published on. REQUIRED IN
# DNS MODE: set it to vault-core's own address (the one your rewrite
# answers), or HTTPS to rewritten names keeps failing. UNSET = not
# published to the LAN at all (Docker binds a random port on 127.0.0.1, so
# it can never clash with a NAS web UI or reverse proxy already on 443).
# It does NOT follow VAULT_CORE_BIND on purpose. For DNS mode, set it to the
# SAME dedicated address as VAULT_CORE_BIND -- the address your DNS rewrite
# points at:
#     VAULT_CORE_BIND=192.168.1.50
#     VAULT_TLS_BIND=192.168.1.50
# 0.0.0.0 (all interfaces) only if you write it here yourself.
#VAULT_TLS_BIND=
#
# Host port for 443, only used when VAULT_TLS_BIND is set. Testing only:
# clients always connect to 443, exactly like port 80 above.
#VAULT_TLS_PORT=443


# Dedicated cache mount. Default (unset/empty): the cache lives in the
# Docker-managed named volume `vault-cache`, same as always -- leave this
# commented out and nothing changes.
#
# Set it to an ABSOLUTE host path to bind-mount that directory at /vault in
# BOTH vault-core and vault-api instead -- e.g. a dedicated ZFS dataset on a
# big HDD pool (deploy/examples/truenas-scale-dockge.md has the full recipe).
# It must be an absolute path (start with /): Compose's volume short-syntax
# treats anything NOT starting with "/", "./" or "~/" as a NAMED VOLUME
# reference instead of a bind-mount path. This fails loudly, not silently --
# verified: `VAULT_CACHE_PATH=mycache` makes `docker compose config` refuse
# with `service "vault-core" refers to undefined volume mycache: invalid
# compose project`, because only `vault-cache` is declared under the
# top-level `volumes:` key. A typo here cannot come up half-working.
# In this mode vault-api's mount drops its named-volume-only `nocopy` option
# by itself: Docker 28 (observed 28.3.1) refuses a bind that carries it
# ("field VolumeOptions must not be specified"), so an older compose.yaml
# must be updated.
#
# Requirements, both enforced at container boot by vault-core's preflight
# (core/docker/40-vault-preflight.sh), not merely suggested:
#   1. Ownership: `mkdir -p <path>/cache/depot <path>/tmp && chown 101:101
#      <path>/cache/depot` (as root) BEFORE first start. uid/gid 101 is the
#      nginx worker user and owns only the depot tree; <path>, cache/, tmp/
#      and logs/ stay root:root 0755, which vault-core enforces (and migrates
#      from an older `chown -R 101:101`) at every start (WP SEC-FIX-5). Wrong
#      owners make vault-core refuse to boot with the exact commands to run.
#   2. Same filesystem: this path becomes the single /vault mount for both
#      cache/ and tmp/ together -- never point it at something that itself
#      spans two filesystems/mounts (e.g. don't separately bind-mount a
#      different device over <path>/tmp afterwards). proxy_store finishes
#      every cached object with an atomic rename() from tmp/ into
#      cache/depot/..., which only works within one filesystem
#      (core/README.md "Same-filesystem requirement"); vault-core compares
#      st_dev at every boot and refuses to start if it ever finds two.
#VAULT_CACHE_PATH=/mnt/tank/steamhangar-cache


# Cache-event log (WP 3.10/3.11, ADR-0008; core/README.md "Cache-event log").
# DEFAULT ON as of the 2026-08-17 packaging work package (docs/PROJECT_PLAN.md
# §7 Phase 5): earlier phases shipped this off because nothing consumed it
# yet. WP 3.11 shipped that consumer -- miss-triggered prefill completion,
# per-client hit statistics, and bypass detection (requirement A12) -- so
# there is no longer a reason to make an operator opt in by hand.
#
# TWO variables, because there are two sides of the one file:
#   VAULT_EVENT_LOG      -- vault-core's WRITE path. nginx appends one
#                            tab-separated v1 line per depot/manifest
#                            request (core/README.md documents all 9 fields).
#   VAULT_EVENT_LOG_PATH -- vault-api's READ path. WP 3.11's sweeper tails
#                            this file from a persisted byte-offset cursor.
# They MUST be the SAME absolute path on the shared /vault volume, or
# vault-api silently reads nothing: the miss-trigger never fires, `GET
# /v1/clients` never reports a bypass, and `GET /v1/stats` stays at zero --
# no error, just a feature that looks enabled and does nothing (exactly the
# class of bug this packaging work package closed; see LEARNINGS.md
# "Containers"). No extra volume/mount is needed for either side: both
# vault-core and vault-api already mount the identical /vault volume (named
# volume, or the same VAULT_CACHE_PATH above).
VAULT_EVENT_LOG=/vault/logs/event.log
VAULT_EVENT_LOG_PATH=/vault/logs/event.log
#
# Stated honestly, growth: this is ONE TSV LINE PER REQUEST, not per
# cache-chunk byte -- small per line, but genuinely unbounded over the life
# of the deployment. In the shipped containers vault-api may READ this file
# but has no permission to TRUNCATE it (that would race vault-core's own
# writer, which keeps the file handle open) -- WP 3.11's sweeper treats an
# unreadable/rotated file as a counted, surfaced condition (`GET /v1/stats`),
# never a crash, but nothing here rotates the file FOR you. If disk space
# matters, that is on you: e.g. a cron job that truncates
# /vault/logs/event.log once the sweeper's cursor has caught up, or simply
# deleting it outright (the persisted cursor resets cleanly against a
# missing file, same as a fresh install -- it does not need the old bytes).
#
# Must be an absolute path under /vault/ -- vault-core's
# core/docker/25-vault-eventlog.sh hook validates this at container start
# (strict character allowlist plus the /vault/ prefix requirement) and
# refuses to boot rather than start half-configured; anything outside /vault/
# is rejected because this value is written verbatim into nginx.conf and the
# hook and nginx's root master act on that path. Every directory on the way
# must belong to root (WP SEC-FIX-5); /vault/logs/ does.
#
# To go back to the pre-2026-08-17 off default: set BOTH to empty ("").
# VAULT_EVENT_LOG= makes vault-core strip the access_log directives entirely
# (no file is ever created or written); an empty VAULT_EVENT_LOG_PATH makes
# `config.event_sweep_enabled` False, so the sweeper does no work at all
# (api/README.md "Cache event sweep").


# =============================================================================
# vault-api (control plane)
# =============================================================================

# Which host address vault-api's port 8080 is published on. Default: 0.0.0.0.
# LAN-only either way: to reach the API from outside your network use Tailscale,
# Twingate, or your own TLS reverse proxy -- never a port forward
# (docs/PROJECT_PLAN.md §10 "Remote access").
#VAULT_API_BIND=0.0.0.0
#VAULT_API_PORT=8080

# Log level: DEBUG, INFO, WARNING, ERROR. Default: INFO. Read by BOTH
# vault-api and vault-runner (WP S-2) -- one operator-facing knob for the
# whole stack's verbosity, not two.
#VAULT_LOG_LEVEL=INFO

# Hard time budget for a single SteamPrefill run, in seconds. A hang backstop,
# not a performance knob -- a large game on a slow line legitimately takes hours.
# Default: 14400 (4 h). Read by BOTH vault-api (subprocess mode) and
# vault-runner (queue mode, WP S-2) -- whichever process actually invokes
# SteamPrefill is the one this timeout bounds; set one value, both sides agree.
#VAULT_PREFILL_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=14400

# How many chunk requests one SteamPrefill run keeps in flight (its hidden
# --max-threads flag, passed on every prefill). Whole number 1-64, anything
# else refuses to boot. Default: 8 (SteamPrefill's own default is 30).
# Every chunk vault-core fetches opens a new upstream connection, so behind a
# carrier-grade NAT (DS-Lite, many mobile and fibre lines) 30 parallel
# requests plus SteamPrefill's instant retries can exhaust the NAT's port
# mappings: downloads stall and vault-core logs
# "connect() failed (113: Host is unreachable)". Lower it (e.g. 4) if that
# still happens; raise it towards 30 on a line with its own public IPv4.
# Read by BOTH vault-api (subprocess mode) and vault-runner (queue mode),
# like the timeout above.
#VAULT_PREFILL_MAX_THREADS=8

# How long the job worker sleeps between polls of an empty queue. Default: 1.0.
# vault-api only -- in queue mode this is also the interval vault-api's worker
# polls for a queue-mode job's result (api/README.md "Queue mode"); the
# runner's OWN idle-poll interval is the separate VAULT_RUNNER_POLL_SECONDS
# below.
#VAULT_WORKER_POLL_SECONDS=1.0


# --- The SteamPrefill runner split (WP S-2, ADR-0012) -----------------------
#
# `deploy/compose.yaml` runs SteamPrefill in its OWN container (`vault-runner`)
# rather than inside vault-api's process, so vault-api's container can later
# be locked down to LAN-only egress (EG-1) without also cutting off the one
# thing that legitimately needs the wider internet. This is why the one-time
# interactive login moved too -- see "First run" in deploy/README.md, now
# `docker exec` into the `vault-runner` container instead of `vault-api`.
#
# VAULT_PREFILL_MODE switches how vault-api hands prefill jobs off:
#   subprocess -- vault-api runs SteamPrefill itself, in-process (no separate
#                 runner needed at all -- this is what api/README.md's
#                 bare-metal/native dev setup uses, and what vault-api falls
#                 back to on its own if this variable is ever unset).
#   queue      -- vault-api hands a job off through the database instead, and
#                 the vault-runner container claims and executes it.
# `deploy/compose.yaml` SHIPS `queue` here -- a deliberate difference from
# vault_api/config.py's own built-in default (`subprocess`), which only
# applies when this variable is unset entirely (the native/bare-metal path
# above). Set on BOTH services, and both need it: vault-api to know it must
# hand off rather than call SteamPrefill directly, vault-runner because its
# own settings loader validates the same variable (harmless there: nothing in
# the runner's own code branches on it, but a typo here is a security-
# relevant misconfiguration either way -- see api/README.md "Queue mode").
# Refused at startup (both services) if set to anything other than
# `subprocess`/`queue`.
#
# SWITCHING TO `subprocess` HERE IS NOT ENOUGH BY ITSELF (review round 2,
# should-fix S5). deploy/compose.yaml's vault-api service no longer mounts
# /opt/steamprefill/Config (WP S-2 moved that volume to vault-runner
# unconditionally, regardless of this variable's value) -- so with
# VAULT_PREFILL_MODE=subprocess alone, vault-api tries to run SteamPrefill
# itself again but has no persistent place to keep the logged-in session:
# every prefill job fails with a "log in first" message, forever, in a
# loop, with no hint pointing at this file as the cause. If you need
# `subprocess` mode with this shipped compose file, you must ALSO restore
# vault-api's Config/ mount yourself via a compose.override.yaml (see
# deploy/README.md "First run" for the exact volume/path) -- this is not
# the supported, tested shape of this compose file; `queue` (the default
# above) is.
VAULT_PREFILL_MODE=queue

# How stale vault-runner's lease (its last heartbeat while executing a job)
# must be before vault-api's worker gives up waiting on it and fails the job
# instead. vault-api-only -- vault-runner writes the heartbeat on its own
# cadence (VAULT_RUNNER_HEARTBEAT_SECONDS below); deciding when a heartbeat
# counts as stale is the waiting side's call, not the writer's. Several
# multiples of the heartbeat interval on purpose (a single missed heartbeat
# under a GC pause or a slow disk fsync must not read as a dead process --
# docs/adr/0012-prefill-runner-split.md §4 has the full margin reasoning).
# Must be > 0. Default: 30.0.
#VAULT_RUNNER_LEASE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=30.0

# How often vault-runner refreshes its lease while a job is running.
# vault-runner-only. Must be > 0. Default: 5.0.
#VAULT_RUNNER_HEARTBEAT_SECONDS=5.0

# How often vault-runner polls the job queue for work when idle.
# vault-runner-only -- vault-api's OWN wait-on-hand-off poll interval is
# VAULT_WORKER_POLL_SECONDS above, a deliberately separate setting even
# though the two defaults happen to start out close (api/README.md "Queue
# mode" explains why they are not shared). Must be > 0. Default: 1.0.
#VAULT_RUNNER_POLL_SECONDS=1.0


# TTL in seconds for the in-process per-game size cache. Must be > 0 (vault-api
# refuses to start with 0). Invalidated immediately after a prefill or a
# deletion, so a longer TTL does not make those look stale. Default: 60.
#VAULT_SIZE_CACHE_TTL=60

# How many installed-games snapshots vault-api keeps per reporting client
# (vault-agent posts the full list each time; older rows are pruned).
# Must be >= 2 — the removal diff compares the new snapshot against the previous
# one, so keeping fewer would break it; vault-api refuses to start below that,
# it does not silently round up. Default: 20 (~10 h of history at the agent's
# default reporting interval, and still tiny on disk).
#VAULT_AGENT_REPORT_KEEP=20

# How many archived manifest .bin files prune_archive keeps PER DEPOT (WP 3.2,
# api/README.md "Manifest archive") -- the current manifest plus its
# predecessors, "keep the last N" semantics. Must be >= 1 (0 would mean "keep
# nothing", defeating archiving entirely). Default: 3.
#VAULT_MANIFEST_KEEP=3

# Event-sweep tuning (WP 3.11, ADR-0008) -- the knobs behind the cache-event
# log's consumer (see VAULT_EVENT_LOG_PATH above). All optional; defaults
# match config.py's own built-in defaults byte-for-byte, so leaving every one
# of these commented out is identical to today's behaviour.

# How often the sweeper tails the event log, in minutes. Runs unconditionally
# on its own cadence (independent of VAULT_SCHEDULE_WINDOW) -- see
# api/README.md "Why the sweep ignores the schedule window". Must be >= 1.
# Default: 5 (comfortably coarser than vault-core's flush=5s log buffer, so a
# line is never missed for being unflushed).
#VAULT_EVENT_SWEEP_INTERVAL_MINUTES=5

# Per-app cooldown for the miss-triggered prefill, in minutes (ADR-0008: "a
# per-app cooldown so a busy download night cannot enqueue storms").
# `0` is the DEDICATED off switch for the trigger only -- not "no cooldown".
# Statistics and bypass detection keep working with the trigger disabled;
# they are the other half of the sweep. Default: 60.
#VAULT_MISS_TRIGGER_COOLDOWN_MINUTES=60

# Hard cap on how many prefill jobs ONE sweep may enqueue from cache misses --
# bounds a LAN-party burst (several machines each starting a different game
# at once), separately from the per-app cooldown above. Must be >= 1 (use
# VAULT_MISS_TRIGGER_COOLDOWN_MINUTES=0 to turn the trigger off entirely, not
# this). Default: 5.
#VAULT_MISS_TRIGGER_MAX_PER_SWEEP=5

# How many days `GET /v1/clients` looks back for a client's cache-log presence
# before calling it bypass_suspected. 3 rather than 1 on purpose: Steam's own
# LAN P2P transfers can legitimately replace cache traffic for a day, and a
# machine that simply didn't launch Steam yesterday isn't evidence of
# anything (ADR-0001 production requirement 7). Must be >= 1. Default: 3.
#VAULT_BYPASS_WINDOW_DAYS=3

# How many per-sweep statistics rows are kept per client address (same
# bounded-retention shape as VAULT_AGENT_REPORT_KEEP). Must be >= 1. Default:
# 48 (roughly the last 4 hours of fine-grained history at the default 5-minute
# sweep interval; GET /v1/clients' totals sum over the RETAINED rows only).
#VAULT_CLIENT_STATS_KEEP=48

# Truncate the event log once it reaches at least this many bytes (api/README.md
# "Rotation -- best-effort..."). `0` disables truncation attempts entirely.
# Read this before touching it: **in the shipped containers vault-api cannot
# actually truncate the file at all** -- it is vault-core's `nginx` user's
# 0644 file in a 0755 directory, and vault-api's `os.truncate` call raises
# `PermissionError`. Sweeping and correctness are unaffected either way (the
# cursor is committed before truncation is attempted), but every sweep past
# this size logs a WARNING and increments `truncate_denied_count`
# (`GET /v1/stats`) FOREVER if you leave the default in place and never fix
# the permission on vault-core's side. Setting this to `0` is the honest way
# to silence that noise without pretending rotation is happening -- it does
# not make the file smaller, it just stops the repeated denied attempts.
# Must be >= 0. Default: 67108864 (64 MiB, a few hundred thousand lines).
#VAULT_EVENT_LOG_MAX_BYTES=67108864

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Nightly schedule window (WP 3.5, plan §7 Phase 3; forwarded here since
# WP SWEEP-1 / ADR-0014, operator decision after the review's S3 finding).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE TIMEZONE FIRST, BECAUSE IT DECIDES WHAT "LOCAL" MEANS BELOW.
#
# vault-api's scheduler interprets the window below on the CONTAINER's own
# local wall clock (vault_api/scheduler.py's `local_now()`) -- and a
# container's local time IS whatever `TZ` says, nothing more mysterious than
# that. With TZ unset (this file's own default), the container's local time
# is UTC, which means the window below opens at 03:00 UTC, NOT 03:00 in your
# own timezone.
#
# DELIBERATELY UTC, NOT A GUESSED POPULATED ZONE: this is a public project
# shipped to operators everywhere, and defaulting to e.g. `Europe/Berlin`
# would be silently wrong for most of them -- UTC is the one value that is
# honestly correct for everyone by construction, at the cost of the window
# below not actually landing at 3 AM local unless YOU set this. Replace the
# commented default below with your own IANA zone name (see
# https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_tz_database_time_zones for the full
# list) -- for example, for Berlin:
#   TZ=Europe/Berlin
# Default: UTC.
#TZ=UTC
#
# A WRONG TIMEZONE HERE IS MADE VISIBLE, NOT SILENT: vault-api logs one line
# at startup naming the resolved offset/zone abbreviation and the next window
# opening in BOTH local and UTC time, and the REQUESTED TZ value alongside
# the resolved one so a typo is visible (`scheduler.describe_resolved_
# schedule`, logged once on the scheduler's first tick -- see
# api/README.md "Scheduler" / "Timezone"). Check that line after any change
# here -- if it does not say what you expect, TZ is not doing what you think.
#
# The daytime (or overnight) window in which vault-api keeps the gaming
# machines' installed AND cached games current on its own (plan A7/A8, WP 4d,
# WP SWEEP-1). `03:00-07:00` is a SUGGESTED quiet-hours default, not a claim
# about any particular operator's actual gaming schedule -- it is in the
# `TZ` above, so read the two together. Format: HH:MM-HH:MM, zero-padded,
# 24-hour, START INCLUSIVE / END EXCLUSIVE (see api/README.md "Scheduler" for
# the overnight-wrap and '24:00'-as-end-only rules). LEAVE THIS EMPTY TO
# DISABLE THE SCHEDULER ENTIRELY, restoring the pre-2026-08-22 behaviour
# (vault-api only prefills when asked, via POST /v1/prefill or the "check &
# update" buttons) -- this is what every version before WP SWEEP-1 shipped by
# default, on this file's own former silence about this variable. THIS ACTUALLY
# WORKS (review round 2, blocker R2-B1, measured): `deploy/compose.yaml`
# forwards this key with the no-colon substitution form specifically so a
# present-but-blank value here is NOT overwritten by the 03:00-07:00
# default -- the more common colon form would have silently kept the
# scheduler running, which was measured and caught before this shipped.
#
# THE AUTO-GC PAIRING BELOW APPLIES REGARDLESS OF WHETHER THIS WINDOW IS SET:
# unlike the sweep half of VAULT_SWEEP_INCLUDE_CACHED (which only matters
# once a sweep actually runs), VAULT_AUTO_GC=execute below fires after ANY
# successful, update-producing prefill -- manual, miss-triggered, or
# scheduled. Blanking this variable does not blank that one.
#
# Also settable at runtime via PATCH /v1/settings ("schedule_window",
# ADR-0009) -- see api/README.md "Persisted settings".
# Default: 03:00-07:00.
#VAULT_SCHEDULE_WINDOW=03:00-07:00

# --- Upstream (Steam -> vault) rate cap, vault-core ---------------------------
# ONE aggregate limit for all cache-MISS downloads, bytes/s, nginx size syntax:
# k = 1024, m = 1048576 (800k = 819,200 B/s, about 6.5 Mbit/s). Empty = no cap.
# Divided at download time by the number of requests vault-core is serving.
# Env-only: change it, then recreate vault-core. An invalid value stops vault-core.
# (WP TH-1a/TH-1b, ADR-0015; limits in core/README.md "Upstream rate cap".)
VAULT_UPSTREAM_RATE=
# HH:MM-HH:MM (VAULT_SCHEDULE_WINDOW grammar): full speed INSIDE, capped outside,
# in vault-core's TZ (the TZ above, forwarded to vault-core too). Leave this
# line commented to follow VAULT_SCHEDULE_WINDOW above (default 03:00-07:00;
# a blank VAULT_SCHEDULE_WINDOW= then caps around the clock, and a window set
# only via PATCH /v1/settings is NOT followed); uncomment it EMPTY to cap
# around the clock.
#VAULT_UPSTREAM_RATE_WINDOW=

# --- Upstream edge and connection cap, vault-core (WP CORE-FIX-4, ADR-0021) --
# ON by default. Every cache MISS is dialled on ONE pooled Steam edge name over
# keepalive connections (whatever Host the client sent), and a global cap C
# bounds the concurrent upstream connections: over it vault-core answers 503
# (the client retries) instead of exhausting a carrier-grade NAT's port quota
# (`connect() failed (113: Host is unreachable)`).
# The shipped default is the edge the Steam client's discovery marker maps to.
# For a geo-correct name ask your resolver what the marker resolves to:
#   dig +short lancache.steamcontent.com
# and use the edge name your line is sent to (dist-fra1.discovery.steamserver.net
# is the German one). Exactly ONE lowercase name ending in .steamcontent.com or
# .steamserver.net. Env-only: recreate vault-core after a change
# (`docker compose up -d vault-core`).
# ROLLBACK to the older per-name pool below: set it EMPTY (not commented out --
# unset means the default above):
# VAULT_UPSTREAM_EDGE=
# The cap C: whole number 1..64, no off switch, empty = 16. It must be at least
# VAULT_PREFILL_MAX_THREADS (default 8) or vault-core refuses to boot.
#VAULT_UPSTREAM_MAX_CONNS=16
# RECOMMENDATION while you roll this out: keep VAULT_UPSTREAM_RATE=4m and an
# EMPTY VAULT_UPSTREAM_RATE_WINDOW= (throttle round the clock, above) until
# measurements after the rollout show the edge and the cap are stable. Only then
# loosen the throttle (deploy/README.md "Upstream edge and connection cap").

# --- Upstream keepalive pool, vault-core (WP CORE-FEAT-1, ADR-0017) ---------
# LEGACY MODE ONLY: used when VAULT_UPSTREAM_EDGE is set EMPTY (rollback, above).
# While an edge is set, this list is ignored (vault-core logs one line saying so).
# Space-separated Steam CDN edge host names. For each one vault-core keeps a
# pool of idle upstream connections and reuses them across cache MISSes
# instead of opening a new TCP connection per chunk -- the fix for lines
# behind a carrier-grade NAT (DS-Lite), whose port quota a prefill otherwise
# exhausts (`connect() failed (113: Host is unreachable)`). Edges NOT listed
# keep the old one-connection-per-chunk path: no error, just no gain.
# SHIPPED SEED (ADR-0017 decision 2B): the first name is the edge the Steam
# client's discovery marker (lancache.steamcontent.com) maps to; the second was
# measured on a DS-Lite line on 2026-10-02 (deploy/README.md "Checking that a
# Steam edge keeps connections alive"). Replace or extend it with the edges
# YOUR line uses -- with VAULT_EVENT_LOG on (below, the shipped default), after
# a few downloads:
#   docker compose exec vault-core sh -c 'cut -f8 /vault/logs/event.log | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn'
# lists them, most used first. Rules (vault-core refuses to start otherwise,
# `28-vault-upstream-pool.sh: FATAL` in `docker compose logs vault-core`):
# lowercase, no scheme or port, each ending in .steamcontent.com or
# .steamserver.net (the Host-allowlist families), not the marker
# lancache.steamcontent.com itself, no duplicates, AT MOST 4 NAMES (8 idle
# connections per edge, 32 in total). Empty = no pool. Env-only: after a
# change, recreate vault-core with `docker compose up -d vault-core`.
# (Details and limits: deploy/README.md "Upstream keepalive pool".)
VAULT_UPSTREAM_POOL_HOSTS=dist-fra1.discovery.steamserver.net cache6-ams1.steamcontent.com

# Manifest-based garbage collection (api/README.md "The recently-stored grace
# window"): days a freshly stored chunk is protected from GC purely by its own
# store time, regardless of whether it appears in any known manifest. Exists
# for content GC cannot otherwise account for -- an opt-in Steam beta branch,
# a demo, or any build fetched against a manifest vault-api never recorded --
# so a tester's overnight download doesn't get collected the next morning.
# `0` disables the window entirely (GC behaves exactly as if this setting did
# not exist). Must be >= 0. Default: 14.
#VAULT_GC_GRACE_DAYS=14

# Automatic GC after a prefill that actually updated something (WP 3.12,
# api/README.md "Auto-GC"). Queues a GC job in the given mode once a prefill's
# parsed summary reports updated > 0 -- a run that only confirmed "up to date"
# never queues one.
#   off      -- never queue GC automatically (a feature that deletes files
#               does not switch itself on -- this was the default through
#               WP 3.12)
#   dry-run  -- queue a dry-run GC job (preview only, nothing deleted)
#   execute  -- queue a real GC job (deletes orphaned chunks) -- THE DEFAULT
#               since 2026-08-22 (WP SWEEP-1, docs/adr/0014-sweep-cached-and-
#               auto-gc-default-on.md, operator decision)
# VAULT_GC_GRACE_DAYS still applies in execute mode: chunks the prefill itself
# just stored are protected by their own store time, same as any other run.
#
# WHY THE DEFAULT IS NOW execute, NOT off: this setting is PAIRED with
# VAULT_SWEEP_INCLUDE_CACHED below, and the pairing is not symmetric -- see
# that setting's own comment for the full argument. Shipping the cached-apps
# sweep mode on by default WITHOUT also shipping real collection on by
# default is exactly the "keeps itself current straight into a full disk"
# condition this project's own scheduler.cached_sweep_gc_risk warns about;
# pairing the two defaults is what keeps that from being the out-of-the-box
# experience. See the UPGRADE NOTE near the top of this file for what changes
# on an existing deployment that never set this variable, and set this back
# to `off` (see also VAULT_SWEEP_INCLUDE_CACHED=false below) to restore the
# pre-2026-08-22 behavior.
#VAULT_AUTO_GC=execute

# Opt-in manifest oracle (WP 3.9, ADR-0007 addendum B; api/README.md "The
# manifest oracle"). Pre-emptively checks whether a cached app's Steam branch
# manifest has changed, without waiting for the next scheduled prefill sweep.
# OFF by default (empty) -- and unlike the event log above, it should STAY
# off unless you specifically want this, because of what turning it on does:
#
# PRIVACY NOTE: enabling it means vault-api makes outbound HTTPS requests,
# per cached app, on a schedule, to a THIRD PARTY -- api.steamcmd.net by
# default, an unofficial community mirror of Steam's PICS app info, not
# operated by Valve or this project. Those requests reveal which Steam app
# IDs this vault has cached to that third party. If that is unacceptable,
# leave this off (the default), or point VAULT_MANIFEST_ORACLE_URL below at
# your own private mirror/proxy of the same API instead (api/README.md
# documents the response shape it expects) -- doing so REQUIRES setting it
# here; deploy/compose.yaml forwards this variable specifically so that
# instruction is actually reachable (packaging-WP B1 fix: earlier this
# advice existed in this file while the variable itself was silently
# unforwarded, so following it had no effect).
#
# The one implemented value today is "steamcmd_api"; anything else (a typo
# included) makes vault-api refuse to start with an explanation, rather than
# silently running with the oracle off.
#VAULT_MANIFEST_ORACLE=steamcmd_api

# The oracle's base URL -- see the PRIVACY NOTE above. Only the scheme is
# validated (http/https); the host is entirely your choice, including a LAN
# proxy/mirror that never leaves your network. Checked at startup even when
# the oracle is off, same reasoning as every other numeric setting here: a
# typo surfaces immediately, not the day you flip VAULT_MANIFEST_ORACLE on.
# Default: https://api.steamcmd.net/v1/info.
#VAULT_MANIFEST_ORACLE_URL=https://api.steamcmd.net/v1/info

# Per-request timeout for oracle queries, in seconds. Must be a positive,
# finite number (nan/inf are rejected, not silently accepted). Default: 10.0.
#VAULT_MANIFEST_ORACLE_TIMEOUT=10.0


# Per-attempt delivery timeout for the webhook feature (WP 3.13,
# api/README.md "Webhooks"), in seconds. Unlike `VAULT_WEBHOOK_URL` and
# `VAULT_WEBHOOK_EVENTS` (further down, under "Settings-API keys reachable
# from this file"), this timeout has no `PATCH /v1/settings` path at all, so
# it is an env-only knob. Must be a positive, finite number. Default: 5.0.
#VAULT_WEBHOOK_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=5.0

# Sweep target-set mode (WP 4d, api/README.md "Sweep target set - installed
# PLUS cached"). Widens the nightly sweep to ALSO include every app that
# holds content of its OWN on disk (a depot no other tracked app also maps --
# a depot SHARED with another app does not count, api/README.md explains
# why), not only what agent reports say is currently installed -- the fix for
# a game that sits in the cache but is installed nowhere, or whose only PC
# has gone quiet.
#
# ON BY DEFAULT since 2026-08-22 (WP SWEEP-1, docs/adr/0014-sweep-cached-and-
# auto-gc-default-on.md, operator decision) -- was OFF by default through
# WP 4d. The cost that made WP 4d ship this off is real and has not gone
# away: it IS spending bandwidth (checking) and, on real updates, disk (fresh
# chunks) on games nobody currently asked for. It is cheap to turn on in the
# bandwidth sense (a non-forced prefill of an already-current app is a ~3 s,
# zero-byte no-op, ADR-0006) -- what makes accepting the disk cost as the
# shipped default DEFENSIBLE rather than reckless is that it no longer ships
# alone, see the next paragraph.
#
# `VAULT_SWEEP_INCLUDE_CACHED` IS now settable from this file, unlike through
# WP 4d/WP P1: it used to be DB-overridable ONLY, via `PATCH /v1/settings`
# ("sweep_include_cached", ADR-0009), which was fine while its default was
# the safe `off` value. That stopped being enough the moment the default
# became `true`: an operator running the documented, supported
# `VAULT_SETTINGS_READONLY=1` headless/GitOps mode (see that setting below)
# has `PATCH` refused with `403` and, without a line here, no way to turn the
# now-default-on cached sweep back off at all. This line closes that gap.
# `PATCH /v1/settings` remains the OTHER supported way to change it once the
# stack is up, from the web UI / app Settings screen, or `curl -X PATCH
# .../v1/settings` -- both paths coexist (ADR-0009's db > env > default
# precedence is unaffected by this line existing).
#
# COUPLES WITH VAULT_AUTO_GC ABOVE, AND THE COUPLING IS NOT SYMMETRIC: every
# kept-current game this mode refreshes leaves its superseded chunks as fresh
# orphans, so turning this on only pays off if something also collects them.
# `VAULT_AUTO_GC=dry-run` reports what would be reclaimed but reclaims
# NOTHING -- only `execute` actually frees the disk. This is exactly why
# VAULT_AUTO_GC's own default flipped to `execute` in the SAME change as this
# setting's default flipped to `true` -- shipping one without the other is
# the "keeps itself current straight into a full disk" condition this
# project's own scheduler.cached_sweep_gc_risk warns about, not a smaller,
# safer step. Turning this mode on while `VAULT_AUTO_GC` is anything other
# than `execute` is still not refused (the operator may plan to run
# `POST /v1/cache/{appid}/gc` by hand instead), but it is never silent:
# vault-api logs a one-time WARNING on that combination and
# `GET /v1/schedule`'s `sweep_cached_gc_risk` field exposes the identical
# condition for a UI to flag too.
#
# See the UPGRADE NOTE near the top of this file for what changes on an
# existing deployment that never set this variable, and set this to `false`
# (together with VAULT_AUTO_GC=off above) to restore the pre-2026-08-22
# behavior.
#VAULT_SWEEP_INCLUDE_CACHED=true

# Steam relay privacy gate (WP 4h.0, ADR-0010; api/README.md "The privacy
# gate"). Two independent switches -- one per field -- for whether
# `GET /v1/steam/owned-games` may include `playtime_forever` /
# `rtime_last_played` in its response AT ALL. Both OFF by default: a
# shared-household vault should not surface either without an explicit
# opt-in (docs/PROJECT_PLAN.md Phase 4h's privacy stance). When a switch is
# off, the JSON key is OMITTED entirely, not sent as `0`/`null`.
#
# LIKE `VAULT_SWEEP_INCLUDE_CACHED`/`VAULT_AUTO_GC` just above, these two are
# forwarded by `deploy/compose.yaml` directly -- but UNLIKE them, these two
# deliberately have NO `PATCH /v1/settings` override at all -- the settings
# table lives in the `vault-db` volume, which can be lost independently of
# this file, and a privacy opt-out must not have "silently starts collecting
# again" as its failure mode. See docs/adr/0010-relay-privacy-gate-env-only.md
# for the
# full argument (including the runtime-toggle design this project
# considered and rejected). Changing either value means editing this file
# (or `deploy/compose.yaml`) and restarting the `vault-api` container --
# there is no runtime switch.
# Default: false (both).
#VAULT_RELAY_EXPOSE_PLAYTIME=false
#VAULT_RELAY_EXPOSE_LAST_PLAYED=false

# Settings-API operator hard-lock (ADR-0009 decision 3; api/README.md "The
# operator hard-lock"). Locks `PATCH /v1/settings` entirely (403 for every
# request, regardless of body) while `GET /v1/settings` keeps working. Off
# (read-write) by default -- a fresh install gets the writable settings API:
# vault name, schedule window/cadence, webhook URL/event filter and the
# auto-GC mode can all be changed at runtime via `PATCH /v1/settings`.
#
# UNLIKE `VAULT_SWEEP_INCLUDE_CACHED`/`VAULT_WEBHOOK_URL` above, this one has
# NO `PATCH /v1/settings` override at all, and by definition never can: a
# flag that disables the settings-write API cannot itself be turned back on
# through that same API (ADR-0009 decision 3). Env forwarding via
# `deploy/compose.yaml` is therefore the ONLY way to reach it -- set it to
# 1/true/yes/on for a locked-down/GitOps deployment that wants the
# pre-ADR-0009 pure-env posture instead, and restart the `vault-api`
# container; there is no runtime switch. Accepted: 1/true/yes/on (locked),
# 0/false/no/off/blank (writable), case-insensitive.
# Default: false.
#VAULT_SETTINGS_READONLY=false

# --- Settings-API keys reachable from this file --------------------------------
#
# The five remaining `PATCH /v1/settings` keys (ADR-0009; `GET /v1/settings`
# lists all of them). Through the pre-freeze project review these five were
# DELIBERATELY absent from this file and from `deploy/compose.yaml`, on the
# reasoning that the settings API (web UI / Android app Settings screen, or
# `curl -X PATCH .../v1/settings`) is the supported way to set them. That
# reasoning has exactly the hole ADR-0014 already closed for
# VAULT_SWEEP_INCLUDE_CACHED and VAULT_SCHEDULE_WINDOW above: a deployment
# running the documented `VAULT_SETTINGS_READONLY=1` hard-lock has every
# PATCH refused with 403, and without a forwarding line these five were
# UNREACHABLE in the shipped stack -- a headless/GitOps vault could never
# be named, never tune its sweep cadence, and never configure a webhook
# (review finding S3). `deploy/compose.yaml` now forwards all five.
#
# Precedence is unchanged (db > env > default, ADR-0009): on a read-write
# deployment a value set via PATCH still wins over a line here, and a line
# here is the STARTUP fallback that applies until someone PATCHes -- which is
# what an infra-as-code first boot wants. Leaving a line commented out (or
# blank) means vault-api's own built-in default applies; there is no
# compose-side default to keep in sync for any of the five.

# Human-readable name for this vault, shown by the web UI / app and included
# in webhook payloads (api/README.md "Settings"). Default: none (unset).
# At most 64 characters after trimming, printable only (no tab or other
# control character); anything else is refused at startup with a clear
# message (and answered 422 on PATCH). Upgrade caveat: an existing name that
# is longer than 64 characters or contains a non-printable character such as
# a tab now stops vault-api from booting until it is fixed.
#VAULT_NAME=

# Minimum spacing between two scheduler sweeps, in minutes (api/README.md
# "Scheduler"). Only meaningful with a VAULT_SCHEDULE_WINDOW set above.
# Positive integer. Default: 180.
#VAULT_SCHEDULE_INTERVAL_MINUTES=180

# A client whose newest agent report is older than this many days drops out
# of the sweep's target set (api/README.md "Scheduler"). Positive integer.
# Default: 7.
#VAULT_SCHEDULE_CLIENT_STALE_DAYS=7

# Webhook receiver for job/sweep events (WP 3.13, api/README.md "Webhooks").
# Blank/unset = the webhook feature is off. NOTE the egress lock below: an
# operator-supplied receiver outside the LAN, or on another LAN device, must
# ALSO be listed in VAULT_EGRESS_ALLOW or every delivery is refused with a
# 403 Filtered by vault-proxy -- see that variable's own comment.
# Default: none (off).
#VAULT_WEBHOOK_URL=

# Comma-separated event-name filter for VAULT_WEBHOOK_URL (api/README.md
# "Webhooks" lists the names). Blank/unset = every event is delivered.
# Default: none (all events).
#VAULT_WEBHOOK_EVENTS=

# The SteamID64 whose library the web UI and the app show (WP API-FEAT-1,
# api/README.md "Persisted settings"). One per vault, so no device has to
# store its own. A public identifier, not a secret. Exactly 17 digits in the
# individual-account range (e.g. 76561197960287930); anything else refuses
# startup with a clear message (and is answered 422 on PATCH). Settable via
# PATCH /v1/settings too; this line is the readonly-deployment path.
# Default: none (not set).
#VAULT_STEAM_LIBRARY_STEAMID=

# --- The egress lock (WP EG-1, ADR-0011) ------------------------------------
#
# As of this update, `deploy/compose.yaml` gives vault-api's own container NO
# default route for an ARBITRARY outbound connection (see the big banner
# comment above that file's top-level `networks:` block for the full
# mechanism). Reaching an arbitrary WAN or other-LAN-device destination
# requires passing through a new `vault-proxy` container, which refuses
# every such destination not in its allowlist. This is DEFAULT-ON in the
# shipped compose file -- there is no toggle to turn it off; see
# deploy/README.md's "Egress lock" section for the documented, supported way
# to remove it (a compose.override.yaml, an explicit act, not an env flag) --
# and for the two narrower channels (DNS resolution; the Docker host's own
# reachable addresses) this lock measurably does NOT close, named there
# rather than implied away.
#
# VAULT_EGRESS_ALLOW is the allowlist's operator-facing half: extra hostnames
# (comma-separated, no scheme, no path, no port -- just the bare host, e.g.
# "api.steamcmd.net,my-webhook-receiver.example.com") vault-proxy will let
# vault-api reach, IN ADDITION TO the one host baked into vault-proxy's own
# image unconditionally (api.steampowered.com, for the Steam Web API relay --
# see deploy/proxy/docker-entrypoint.sh's own comment for why THAT one host
# specifically is not gated behind this variable). Empty by default: the lock
# ships on with an empty allowlist, not a wide-open one.
#
# Forwarded to BOTH vault-api and vault-proxy (identical value, same as
# VAULT_PREFILL_MODE's two-sided forwarding above) -- vault-proxy is the side
# that actually RENDERS the allowlist into its filter file; vault-api's own
# copy is used ONLY to cross-check against VAULT_MANIFEST_ORACLE below, at
# boot, so a misconfiguration is a loud startup failure instead of a filtered
# oracle request that just fails forever with no obvious cause.
#
# TWO real, documented cases need an entry here:
#
#   1. The manifest oracle (VAULT_MANIFEST_ORACLE below). Turning it on
#      WITHOUT adding its host here makes vault-api refuse to boot, with an
#      explicit error naming the missing host -- this is deliberate (the
#      alternative is a silent, permanent filtered-403 on every oracle
#      query). Add the oracle's host -- "api.steamcmd.net" for the default
#      VAULT_MANIFEST_ORACLE_URL, or your own mirror's host if you changed
#      that too.
#
#   2. Webhook receivers (VAULT_WEBHOOK_URL, set via `PATCH /v1/settings`,
#      ADR-0009 -- not in this file at all, see that section elsewhere in
#      this document). If your webhook stops firing after this update, THAT
#      IS THE LOCK WORKING, not a bug: add the receiver's host here. This
#      applies EQUALLY to a receiver on your own LAN (a local ntfy/Home
#      Assistant instance) and one on the internet (Discord, a public ntfy
#      server) -- measured directly (WP EG-1): vault-api's own network has
#      no WORKING direct route to a SEPARATE device once this lock is in
#      effect, LAN or WAN alike, so there is no "skip the proxy for local
#      traffic" case to carve out for that common shape. (One narrow, named
#      exception: a receiver bound to the DOCKER HOST's own address, not a
#      separate device, is reachable directly regardless -- see
#      deploy/README.md's "Egress lock" section, "Two channels this does
#      NOT close".) Every outbound call to a separate device goes through
#      vault-proxy, or it does not go out at all.
#
# See deploy/README.md's "Egress lock" section for the full mechanism and a
# five-minute, tcpdump-based recipe to verify all of this yourself rather
# than trust this comment.
#VAULT_EGRESS_ALLOW=

# VAULT_EGRESS_SUBNET (WP DEPLOY-FIX-2) is the private subnet of the internal
# vault-egress network between vault-api and vault-proxy. Leave it unset: the
# default 172.30.238.0/24 is right for a single stack. You only need it when a
# second SteamHangar stack, or anything else, already uses 172.30.238.0/24 on
# this host -- `docker compose up -d` then fails with "Pool overlaps with other
# one on this address space". In that case pick another private /24 that does
# not overlap ANY existing Docker network on the host (`docker network inspect
# $(docker network ls -q) --format '{{range .IPAM.Config}}{{.Subnet}} {{end}}'`
# lists them), e.g. 172.30.239.0/24. Compose uses the value for the network
# and forwards the same value to vault-proxy, which renders its client `Allow`
# line from it. vault-proxy accepts only a strict IPv4 CIDR (a.b.c.d/n, prefix
# 8-30) and refuses to start on anything else. Blank behaves like unset.
#VAULT_EGRESS_SUBNET=


# =============================================================================
# vault-dns (OPTIONAL -- only with `docker compose --profile dns up -d`)
#
# Skip this whole section unless you have no local DNS server. If you run
# AdGuard Home, Pi-hole, dnsmasq or Unbound, configure the rewrite there instead
# (dns/README.md has copy-paste instructions for each).
# =============================================================================

# REQUIRED when the dns profile is enabled: the LAN IPv4 address of the machine
# running vault-core -- what every *.steamcontent.com A query gets answered with.
# Must match VAULT_CORE_BIND if you set that to a dedicated IP. No default; the
# container fails fast with an explanation if it is empty.
#CACHE_IP=192.168.1.50

# *** THE SINGLE MOST IMPORTANT SECURITY SETTING IN THIS FILE ***
# Which host address port 53 is published on. vault-dns is a forwarding resolver
# with no source-address ACL: reachable from the internet it becomes an open
# resolver, i.e. free DNS amplification/reflection capacity for attackers,
# billed to your bandwidth and your IP's reputation.
# Set it to ONE specific LAN address -- normally the same one as CACHE_IP:
#VAULT_DNS_BIND=192.168.1.50
# Leaving it unset publishes on 127.0.0.1 instead, which fails closed (no LAN
# client can use it, and you will notice) rather than open. Do not set it to
# 0.0.0.0.

# Host port for DNS. Change only for testing alongside an existing resolver.
#VAULT_DNS_PORT=53

# Where vault-dns forwards everything that is NOT *.steamcontent.com.
# Defaults: 1.1.1.1 (Cloudflare) and 8.8.8.8 (Google). Point them at your
# router or ISP resolver if you prefer -- plain IPv4 addresses only.
#UPSTREAM_DNS_1=1.1.1.1
#UPSTREAM_DNS_2=8.8.8.8


# =============================================================================
# Images and logging
# =============================================================================

# Tag on every `image:` line in deploy/compose.yaml -- all five services
# resolve to `ghcr.io/steamhangar/<service>:<this tag>` (vault-runner reuses
# vault-api's image). It serves BOTH deployment styles deploy/README.md
# "Quickstart" describes: with `docker compose pull` it selects the
# published release to fetch from ghcr.io; with `docker compose up -d
# --build` it is the tag the local builds are stored under. Bump it when
# you cut or move to a release so a rebuild cannot accidentally reuse an
# older layer set. Default: the release baked into this checkout.
#VAULT_IMAGE_TAG=0.1.0

# json-file log rotation, applied to every service. Defaults: 10m x 5 files per
# service (~150 MB total worst case). vault-core writes one line per depot
# request, so raise max-file if you want prefill history to survive longer.
#VAULT_LOG_MAX_SIZE=10m
#VAULT_LOG_MAX_FILE=5
